    # IP FORWARDING, for a machine that carries traffic for its tailnet
    # (SPEC.md §spec:tailscale).
    #
    # Appended by render-ignition.sh only when the operator asked this machine to
    # be an exit node or to advertise routes. A machine that joins a tailnet for
    # its own sake does not forward anything and gets no such file: turning on
    # forwarding widens what a machine does with packets that are not addressed
    # to it, and that is not a side effect to hand somebody who asked for a VPN.
    #
    # WITHOUT THIS, THE FEATURE FAILS QUIETLY IN THE WORST WAY. `tailscale up`
    # succeeds, the admin console offers the route for approval, the operator
    # approves it - and every packet sent through this machine is dropped by the
    # kernel. Nothing in Tailscale's status is red. tailscaled does print a
    # warning at the moment it comes up, which is one line in a journal nobody is
    # reading on a headless machine that just installed itself.
    #
    # IPv4 forwarding is very probably already on: Docker sets it when the daemon
    # starts, because it needs it for container networking. That is exactly why
    # it is stated here anyway - it would otherwise be a property this machine
    # borrows from another service's side effect, and a Docker that failed to
    # start would take subnet routing with it for reasons nobody would connect.
    #
    # IPv6 forwarding is NOT already on, and is needed for any IPv6 route and for
    # an exit node carrying IPv6 traffic.
    #
    # /etc/sysctl.d rather than /usr/lib/sysctl.d because this is the operator's
    # choice about this machine, not the image's about every machine - and /etc
    # is what survives to the installed system from here.
    - path: /etc/sysctl.d/99-kantainer-tailscale-forwarding.conf
      mode: 0644
      user:
        id: 0
      group:
        id: 0
      contents:
        inline: |
          # Written because this machine advertises routes or offers itself as an
          # exit node (SPEC.md §spec:tailscale). Without both lines the kernel
          # drops every packet Tailscale asks it to carry, and nothing says so.
          net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1
          net.ipv6.conf.all.forwarding = 1
